WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.373
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4568.

2
00:00:01.373 --> 00:00:02.746
Begin Session IV.]

3
00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:03.203
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:04.576
That looks good.

5
00:00:04.576 --> 00:00:07.780
I'm here today, again, with Mr. M.D.

6
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:08.695
Haki Vincent.

7
00:00:08.695 --> 00:00:10.983
It's March the twenty-fourth, 2016.

8
00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:22.424
This is our fourth interview together, I think, because we interviewed once together at the Rigmaiden Center, and then once with Jamie, and then Kyle.

9
00:00:22.424 --> 00:00:26.085
So yes, this is our fourth interview together.

10
00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:27.000
<v HAKI VINCENT>Okay.

11
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.694
<v ARSENEAULT>So we're in his home on Coach Williams . . . is it Drive or Avenue?

12
00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:32.417
Coach Williams . . .

13
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:33.500
<v VINCENT>Coach Williams Drive.

14
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:33.861
<v ARSENEAULT>Drive.

15
00:00:33.861 --> 00:00:34.222
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

16
00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:34.944
<v ARSENEAULT>Road, whatever.

17
00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:35.306
<v VINCENT>Road.

18
00:00:35.306 --> 00:00:36.389
<v ARSENEAULT>Coach Williams Drive.

19
00:00:36.389 --> 00:00:43.611
And we're here today to talk a little bit about your involvement in the environmental justice activism in Mossville [Louisiana].

20
00:00:43.611 --> 00:00:46.500
So the first question, and to get started,

21
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.278
I know you didn't . . . you didn't grow up here, but you moved here in the '70s.

22
00:00:52.278 --> 00:00:53.000
<v VINCENT>[Agrees]

23
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:04.053
<v ARSENEAULT>And I know that the movement kind of took off in the '90s for sure.

24
00:01:04.053 --> 00:01:17.316
But I wanted to ask you when did you first become aware that there was pollution in Mossville?

25
00:01:17.316 --> 00:01:21.737
<v VINCENT>I would say in the '80s.

26
00:01:21.737 --> 00:01:26.158
I would . . . Around the middle '80s.

27
00:01:26.158 --> 00:01:29.842
<v ARSENEAULT>What specific event kind of . . .

28
00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:35.000
<v VINCENT>Well they had some spills, toxic spills,

29
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:49.737
and the people in the area where it spilled across the tracks were concerned about the health and the conditions.

30
00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:54.895
So that's when it really kicked off.

31
00:01:54.895 --> 00:02:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When you say "across the tracks," what does that mean?

32
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.424
<v VINCENT>That was east of here.

33
00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:10.273
When I say "across the tracks," that's the east part

34
00:02:10.273 --> 00:02:10.758
<c.p>of . . .

35
00:02:10.758 --> 00:02:13.182
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean like Bel Air?

36
00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:18.030
<v VINCENT>Yes, Bel Air area and the east part of Mossville.

37
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

38
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:27.948
<v VINCENT>That's when we were contacted by some people who were interested in exposing it.

39
00:02:27.948 --> 00:02:33.701
And they started what they call a Bucket Brigade.

40
00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:42.010
So we were empowered with the knowledge of how the Bucket Brigade worked.

41
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:52.237
We were empowered with . . . See at that time they had those . . . what did they call them?

42
00:02:52.237 --> 00:02:53.515
Little beeper-like . . .

43
00:02:53.515 --> 00:02:57.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, like a detector . . . or something?

44
00:02:57.351 --> 00:02:57.990
<v VINCENT>No.

45
00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.907
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, a pager?

46
00:02:59.907 --> 00:03:01.186
<v VINCENT>Pager, yes.

47
00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:04.381
We started off with pagers.

48
00:03:04.381 --> 00:03:15.247
So people would call a certain number and let us know that they smelled whatever they smelled.

49
00:03:15.247 --> 00:03:29.948
Shirley and myself were the principal persons who checked on calls that we got from a pager about conditions somewhere in the community.

50
00:03:29.948 --> 00:03:39.536
So whenever they called, we would go out to that area and take a sample.

51
00:03:39.536 --> 00:03:40.814
We had

52
00:03:40.814 --> 00:03:48.485
<c.p>our buckets, and we would take a sample of whatever we smelled.

53
00:03:48.485 --> 00:04:01.907
The sample was mailed to California, to a lab in California, and they would check it and send the results back.

54
00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:12.134
So that would go on any time of night or day that we'd get a call.

55
00:04:12.134 --> 00:04:21.082
We'd have to run out and check and send the samples into the lab.

56
00:04:21.082 --> 00:04:24.278
So that's how we started.

57
00:04:24.278 --> 00:04:54.320
A lot of times the company that we were checking on would call the cops and the cops sought after us or send some of their security out to try to chase us off to keep us from getting samples of the mess that they were dumping.

58
00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:01.990
So that would go on on a regular basis quite a bit.

59
00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:18.608
We were getting a lot of good samples and a lot of results, and they were getting real shaky about us doing what we were doing.

60
00:05:18.608 --> 00:05:25.000
So that was the beginning of the Bucket Brigade.

61
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.094
<v ARSENEAULT>I definitely want to ask you more about that.

62
00:05:32.094 --> 00:05:36.824
But back to like the 1980s.

63
00:05:36.824 --> 00:05:41.553
What was spilled that first time?

64
00:05:41.553 --> 00:05:56.529
<v VINCENT>I think it was dioxin and a lot of other chemicals that they were not revealing what they were.

65
00:05:56.529 --> 00:06:05.200
So it was quite a bit that we don't know about.

66
00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.718
<v ARSENEAULT>When did Mossville's groundwater first get contaminated?

67
00:06:10.718 --> 00:06:20.965
<v VINCENT>I would think . . . I'm thinking it was the early part of the '80s.

68
00:06:20.965 --> 00:06:23.329
<v ARSENEAULT>That same spill?

69
00:06:23.329 --> 00:06:24.118
<v VINCENT>Yes.

70
00:06:24.118 --> 00:06:27.271
<v ARSENEAULT>Or a different spill?

71
00:06:27.271 --> 00:06:30.424
<v VINCENT>No. I'm not sure . . .

72
00:06:30.424 --> 00:06:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

73
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:43.692
<v VINCENT>. . . because it was more than one refinery involved, and all of them are dealing with different chemicals and whatnot.

74
00:06:43.692 --> 00:06:49.231
So we're not sure of just what they were.

75
00:06:49.231 --> 00:06:56.615
But we do know that they were infecting and affecting people's health.

76
00:06:56.615 --> 00:07:05.846
<v ARSENEAULT>What . . . How did the company responsible . . . how did they tell the community what had happened?

77
00:07:05.846 --> 00:07:11.385
<v VINCENT>Well, they started off with a bunch of lies.

78
00:07:11.385 --> 00:07:25.538
I'm not sure if they really ever told the truth about what happened, how it happened, where it happened, and when it happened.

79
00:07:25.538 --> 00:07:31.692
The truth is really not known up to this point.

80
00:07:31.692 --> 00:07:42.769
But we do know a lot of people got sick, a lot of people died from the exposure.

81
00:07:42.769 --> 00:07:48.308
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the lies that they told?

82
00:07:48.308 --> 00:07:55.077
<v VINCENT>Number one, it wasn't them; it was somebody else, another plant.

83
00:07:55.077 --> 00:07:57.538
Or it wasn't toxic.

84
00:07:57.538 --> 00:08:06.154
Whatever lies they could think of to come up with, that's what they told.

85
00:08:06.154 --> 00:08:08.615
So we're not sure.

86
00:08:08.615 --> 00:08:09.846
They are.

87
00:08:09.846 --> 00:08:14.154
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they eventually inform the community?

88
00:08:14.154 --> 00:08:24.000
Like did they go on TV, or did they come out and talk to y'all?

89
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.821
<v VINCENT>Yes, they finally came out and talked to the people.

90
00:08:29.821 --> 00:08:44.373
I think the TV exposed a lot of it, so they didn't have a choice but to finally come out with some kind of excuse.

91
00:08:44.373 --> 00:08:47.866
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

92
00:08:47.866 --> 00:08:53.687
<v VINCENT>A lot of chaos, because it was he-say, she-say, they-say.

93
00:08:53.687 --> 00:09:03.582
It was a lot of that going on, and it's still a lot of it going on.

94
00:09:03.582 --> 00:09:08.821
<v ARSENEAULT>So y'all found out that the groundwater was contaminated.

95
00:09:08.821 --> 00:09:09.403
<v VINCENT>Yes.

96
00:09:09.403 --> 00:09:11.731
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened after that?

97
00:09:11.731 --> 00:09:19.881
<v VINCENT>They started buying people out and giving them just change for what they owned.

98
00:09:19.881 --> 00:09:23.955
They wasn't giving them a fair price.

99
00:09:23.955 --> 00:09:29.776
As far as making them whole again, that wasn't happening.

100
00:09:29.776 --> 00:09:45.493
They were just giving them anything they could get away with to pacify the people and get them out of there so they could get the land.

101
00:09:45.493 --> 00:09:47.821
That was their strategy.

102
00:09:47.821 --> 00:09:51.896
So they knew the land was bad.

103
00:09:51.896 --> 00:09:54.224
A lot of places,

104
00:09:54.224 --> 00:10:07.030
they came in and removed the contaminated dirt so say move . . . remove the contaminated dirt and put some fresh dirt in there.

105
00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:11.687
But still the people wasn't sure about what

106
00:10:11.687 --> 00:10:17.507
<c.p>they were doing and whether it was right or not.

107
00:10:17.507 --> 00:10:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was that dirt taken?

108
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.708
<v VINCENT>We don't know.

109
00:10:22.708 --> 00:10:32.385
We don't know if they put it on somebody else's land or what they did with it,

110
00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:40.354
because they put it in containers, covered containers, and hauled it off to wherever.

111
00:10:40.354 --> 00:10:45.477
So that's another question: where, and what it is?

112
00:10:45.477 --> 00:10:47.185
We don't know.

113
00:10:47.185 --> 00:10:52.877
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the community's . . . I'll just try to like understand.

114
00:10:52.877 --> 00:11:01.985
What do you do when you find out that the water you've been drinking is contaminated?

115
00:11:01.985 --> 00:11:05.969
What steps do you take after that?

116
00:11:05.969 --> 00:11:09.385
<v VINCENT>Well they were closing people's wells.

117
00:11:09.385 --> 00:11:14.508
They were informing people to buy water to drink.

118
00:11:14.508 --> 00:11:17.923
Basically, people were on their own.

119
00:11:17.923 --> 00:11:23.615
They had to spend more money to buy good water.

120
00:11:23.615 --> 00:11:26.462
They couldn't use the water.

121
00:11:26.462 --> 00:11:38.985
The only thing we could use the water for was like washing clothes and whatnot, and that still wasn't a good thing.

122
00:11:38.985 --> 00:11:42.400
Basically, everybody was on their own.

123
00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.385
Everybody had to fend for their self.

124
00:11:46.385 --> 00:11:58.338
And the little money they paid the people for their houses and property didn't do anything but get them into debt.

125
00:11:58.338 --> 00:12:05.738
So all those people that were moved out, they're in debt big time.

126
00:12:05.738 --> 00:12:12.000
So they're worse off than they were in the beginning.

127
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.867
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they get in debt?

128
00:12:15.867 --> 00:12:24.247
<v VINCENT>Because the people, they weren't paid enough money to relocate their selves and

129
00:12:24.247 --> 00:12:26.825
<c.p>still be whole again.

130
00:12:26.825 --> 00:12:34.560
So all those people, basically, that moved are deep in debt again.

131
00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.783
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the water board?

132
00:12:37.783 --> 00:12:40.361
Do you remember that?

133
00:12:40.361 --> 00:12:41.006
<v VINCENT>Yes.

134
00:12:41.006 --> 00:12:55.831
The water board, I guess, were doing what they could to furnish the best water they were able to furnish under the conditions.

135
00:12:55.831 --> 00:13:09.367
But then they moved another water company in that's just as bad, or worse, than the one that was already there.

136
00:13:09.367 --> 00:13:12.590
People's water bills are higher.

137
00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:18.392
The water condition is no better than it was.

138
00:13:18.392 --> 00:13:22.259
It's just chaotic; it's a mess.

139
00:13:22.259 --> 00:13:35.795
They turned a lot of people's water off because they're late paying their bills, and this thing and the next thing.

140
00:13:35.795 --> 00:13:39.663
So it's really bad right now.

141
00:13:39.663 --> 00:13:53.843
I think the best water that's coming here is coming out of DeQuincy [Louisiana], and that's a good way down the road.

142
00:13:53.843 --> 00:13:59.000
So that's where we are right now.

143
00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:11.393
<v ARSENEAULT>So was the water board . . . was that after y'all found out there was contamination in the well water?

144
00:14:11.393 --> 00:14:16.902
<v VINCENT>The water board was there all the while.

145
00:14:16.902 --> 00:14:17.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

146
00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:26.541
<v VINCENT>But then they started finding out the water conditions as time went on.

147
00:14:26.541 --> 00:14:41.000
It got worse because of the spill, and the water was leaking over into the water that's supplied to Mossville.

148
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.774
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was on the water board?

149
00:14:44.774 --> 00:14:47.919
Who kind of started that?

150
00:14:47.919 --> 00:14:49.806
<v VINCENT>Let me see.

151
00:14:49.806 --> 00:14:51.694
Mike [DeLong’s?] family.

152
00:14:51.694 --> 00:14:56.726
I think his father is one of them.

153
00:14:56.726 --> 00:15:01.129
It was about three or four people.

154
00:15:01.129 --> 00:15:14.968
I don't remember all their names right off but it . . . It was about four people on the water board at that time.

155
00:15:14.968 --> 00:15:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What were like their goals for the . . .

156
00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:27.023
<v VINCENT>Their goals were to try to get good water furnished.

157
00:15:27.023 --> 00:15:35.451
Try to get people's well . . . People had a lot of wells then,

158
00:15:35.451 --> 00:15:46.688
and a lot of the wells were becoming contaminated so they were closing the wells up.

159
00:15:46.688 --> 00:15:55.116
That was causing the people to have to buy water to drink.

160
00:15:55.116 --> 00:16:00.033
So that's the way it's been happening.

161
00:16:00.033 --> 00:16:07.758
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of support did they have from like local government?

162
00:16:07.758 --> 00:16:12.674
<v VINCENT>The government was not helping the community.

163
00:16:12.674 --> 00:16:27.423
The government was basically helping the government doing what they do to get more money for what they need money for.

164
00:16:27.423 --> 00:16:32.340
So it was bickering back and forth.

165
00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:48.493
The government and the people were constantly bickering back and forth until they end up buying most of the people out and relocating,

166
00:16:48.493 --> 00:16:54.112
and they relocated their selves in different places.

167
00:16:54.112 --> 00:17:00.433
So they're still suffering from that buyout, so-called "buyout."

168
00:17:00.433 --> 00:17:05.349
The investigation of the . . . who did it,

169
00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:17.991
when they did it and all of that stuff, it's still going on, because we went to Geneva,

170
00:17:17.991 --> 00:17:34.847
Switzerland in the '90s and that's when we filed a complaint against the refineries and the government concerning all of the contamination in Mossville.

171
00:17:34.847 --> 00:17:37.656
But up to the

172
00:17:37.656 --> 00:17:51.000
<c.p>day, I haven't seen any results from all those reports we filed in Geneva, Switzerland in the '90s.

173
00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:58.097
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a report on . . . What kind of report was it?

174
00:17:58.097 --> 00:18:12.935
<v VINCENT>Well it was reports about contamination from the twenty-something refineries surrounding us, and the lack of the government to respond to the reports,

175
00:18:12.935 --> 00:18:21.968
and make the community clear on what's happening, who's doing it, and the like.

176
00:18:21.968 --> 00:18:34.226
That has never come forward, to my knowledge, the response of all of the claims we filed against them.

177
00:18:34.226 --> 00:18:38.742
So it's still a mystery to me.

178
00:18:38.742 --> 00:18:51.000
I don't know about everybody else who were involved, but it's still a mystery about the whole issue.

179
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:51.754
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

180
00:18:51.754 --> 00:18:59.295
I know I think . . . Was it Vista that took their . . .

181
00:18:59.295 --> 00:19:03.066
<v VINCENT>Vista was one of them.

182
00:19:03.066 --> 00:19:03.820
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

183
00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:09.098
<v VINCENT>[Agrees] I think . . . Well, out of twenty-four . . .

184
00:19:09.098 --> 00:19:12.115
<v ARSENEAULT>It's hard to . . . Yes.

185
00:19:12.115 --> 00:19:22.672
<v VINCENT>And out of the twenty-four, everybody's claiming it wasn't us; we didn't do it.

186
00:19:22.672 --> 00:19:37.000
So that's the mystery: who did it, how they did it, when they did it, and the like.

187
00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:47.111
<v ARSENEAULT>So there's a spill, and then the company tried to cover it up . . .

188
00:19:47.111 --> 00:19:47.889
<v VINCENT>Yes.

189
00:19:47.889 --> 00:19:51.778
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . and they lied about it.

190
00:19:51.778 --> 00:20:01.889
How did the different industries gather information about what was going on here?

191
00:20:01.889 --> 00:20:09.667
<v VINCENT>How did they gather information about what was going on?

192
00:20:09.667 --> 00:20:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In Mossville.

193
00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.113
<v VINCENT>Well, that's a good question.

194
00:20:16.113 --> 00:20:33.387
I don't know if I can answer that or not, because they're so deceitful and so crooked that . . . I don't know.

195
00:20:33.387 --> 00:20:49.839
They have a lot of clones, too, who represent them and who will say anything they want them to say.

196
00:20:49.839 --> 00:20:53.952
So that's a tough question.

197
00:20:53.952 --> 00:20:58.065
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Mossville ever spied on?

198
00:20:58.065 --> 00:20:59.710
<v VINCENT>Hell, yes.

199
00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:10.403
People's mailboxes and trash cans been going through by spies of the refineries.

200
00:21:10.403 --> 00:21:23.565
Some of them are paid spies, and some of them are just . . . want to be spies.

201
00:21:23.565 --> 00:21:35.903
So who they are, who paid for them, where they come from, that's more mysteries.

202
00:21:35.903 --> 00:21:44.952
They had quite a few of them who come and go.

203
00:21:44.952 --> 00:21:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did y'all find out you were being spied on?

204
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:58.320
<v VINCENT>That must have been in the '80s, too.

205
00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:04.800
A lot of people still don't know that they've been spied on.

206
00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:07.500
That's still a mystery, too.

207
00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:15.060
They don't know who and how, but they do know they were spied on.

208
00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:19.920
<v ARSENEAULT>So you don't know who . . . what company it was?

209
00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:21.000
<v VINCENT>No.

210
00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:28.910
<v ARSENEAULT>Or the people who were involved or anything like that?

211
00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:42.358
<v VINCENT>Because these companies, they are in position to pay mercenaries for any kind of information they want.

212
00:22:42.358 --> 00:22:45.522
They can do that.

213
00:22:45.522 --> 00:22:54.224
But the community can't afford to pay mercenaries to gather intelligence.

214
00:22:54.224 --> 00:22:58.179
So that's where we are.

215
00:22:58.179 --> 00:23:00.552
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . . Let's see.

216
00:23:00.552 --> 00:23:07.672
I wanted to . . . I was curious to ask you.

217
00:23:07.672 --> 00:23:10.045
What is dioxin?

218
00:23:10.045 --> 00:23:10.836
<v VINCENT>[Pardon]?

219
00:23:10.836 --> 00:23:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is dioxin?

220
00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:30.610
<v VINCENT>Dioxin is chemicals produced by . . . I guess just about any plant you can name around here to . . . It's a deadly chemical.

221
00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:33.774
It's dealing with plastics.

222
00:23:33.774 --> 00:23:36.938
That's the main thing.

223
00:23:36.938 --> 00:23:46.429
It's a deadly plastic chemical that's produced for many purposes around here.

224
00:23:46.429 --> 00:23:50.384
So that's in the air

225
00:23:50.384 --> 00:23:54.339
<c.p>for the people to breathe.

226
00:23:54.339 --> 00:24:06.994
<v ARSENEAULT>When did the community first find out that there . . . that it was here in the air?

227
00:24:06.994 --> 00:24:14.904
<v VINCENT>During the Bucket Brigade investigations, which was quite a few.

228
00:24:14.904 --> 00:24:19.650
That's when it was found out.

229
00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.814
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the reaction?

230
00:24:22.814 --> 00:24:28.350
<v VINCENT>Well, disbelief from a lot of people.

231
00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:42.588
Lies from the refineries, mainly through employees that worked for them who don't want to lose their jobs.

232
00:24:42.588 --> 00:24:57.616
So that's the basis for their so-called investigations and lies about it not being poisonous and all of this.

233
00:24:57.616 --> 00:25:02.362
They even lied to the employees.

234
00:25:02.362 --> 00:25:11.062
They don't tell them what they're handling a lot of times.

235
00:25:11.062 --> 00:25:17.390
They don't need masks and stuff like that.

236
00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:27.672
So a lot of these employees didn't live long after they were exposed.

237
00:25:27.672 --> 00:25:34.000
A lot of them have passed away.

238
00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:40.667
<v ARSENEAULT>If you don't mind me asking, you know what causes . . .

239
00:25:40.667 --> 00:25:42.000
<v VINCENT>Have what?

240
00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:45.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Causes of death, do you . . .

241
00:25:45.333 --> 00:25:58.667
<v VINCENT>Oh, it's a lot of different causes: endometriosis . . . it's a lot of names that I can't recall at this time.

242
00:25:58.667 --> 00:26:04.667
But there's plenty that we don't know about, too.

243
00:26:04.667 --> 00:26:17.333
All we know is that people are disappearing because of the ailments that they're getting . . . receiving from these refineries.

244
00:26:17.333 --> 00:26:26.000
A lot of people bring the poison home with them in their clothes,

245
00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:38.000
because they handling all of this poison and they don't change their clothes when they leave the refinery.

246
00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:51.333
They'll wear them home and expose their children and their family to them, and before you know it they're sick.

247
00:26:51.333 --> 00:26:56.000
So a lot of that's happening, too.

248
00:26:56.000 --> 00:27:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did . . . Do you remember Wilma Subra's work . . .

249
00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:03.421
<v VINCENT>Yes, I do.

250
00:27:03.421 --> 00:27:04.842
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . in the community?

251
00:27:04.842 --> 00:27:05.316
<v VINCENT>[Agrees]

252
00:27:05.316 --> 00:27:09.105
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she a part of the Bucket Brigade?

253
00:27:09.105 --> 00:27:11.000
<v VINCENT>Yes, she was involved.

254
00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.842
Yes, in the Bucket Brigade, too.

255
00:27:13.842 --> 00:27:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you tell us a little more about her and her involvement?

256
00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:25.306
<v VINCENT>She was real busy ever since I've been knowing her.

257
00:27:25.306 --> 00:27:27.429
She was real busy

258
00:27:27.429 --> 00:27:32.735
<c.p>and gathering intelligence far as the activity of the refineries.

259
00:27:32.735 --> 00:27:40.694
So she was getting a lot of information and exposing their corruption quite a bit.

260
00:27:40.694 --> 00:27:43.347
She was very active, yes.

261
00:27:43.347 --> 00:27:51.306
She used to be out there with us on the front line, struggling for righteousness.

262
00:27:51.306 --> 00:27:57.673
She did a lot of work; still doing a lot of work.

263
00:27:57.673 --> 00:28:02.449
She got a lot of information together concerning them.

264
00:28:02.449 --> 00:28:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you tell us a little bit more about the Bucket Brigade, kind of how it started.

265
00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:21.016
<v VINCENT>Well they started . . . I don't remember the names of the people who were involved.

266
00:28:21.016 --> 00:28:26.168
But they started with a group in California.

267
00:28:26.168 --> 00:28:33.251
They designed the buckets and the strategy on getting the information.

268
00:28:33.251 --> 00:28:56.435
So they came here and gave classes to . . . I think it was about three or four of us on how to operate the buckets and what to do with the material once you gathered the intelligence,

269
00:28:56.435 --> 00:29:05.450
send it to the . . . send it to California to a . . . what you call it?

270
00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:12.534
I was getting ready to say a studio, but a company . . .

271
00:29:12.534 --> 00:29:15.110
<v ARSENEAULT>A lab or something?

272
00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:16.398
<v VINCENT>Lab. [laughs]

273
00:29:16.398 --> 00:29:17.042
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

274
00:29:17.042 --> 00:29:22.194
<v VINCENT>Yes, send it to a lab out there.

275
00:29:22.194 --> 00:29:33.785
And the lab sends the results back to MEAN [Mossville Environmental Action Now], the organization that's representing Mossville.

276
00:29:33.785 --> 00:29:34.429
Then

277
00:29:34.429 --> 00:29:47.309
<c.p>they would determine what it was that we picked up, what time it was, where it was, and the like.

278
00:29:47.309 --> 00:30:00.832
So we would be gathering all that information to prove that one refinery or another were dumping poison on the people.

279
00:30:00.832 --> 00:30:04.696
So that's how that went on.

280
00:30:04.696 --> 00:30:13.068
And usually it was with the little beeper . . . what you call them again?

281
00:30:13.068 --> 00:30:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The pager.

282
00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:15.478
<v VINCENT>Pager.

283
00:30:15.478 --> 00:30:21.213
We were using pagers in the beginning, then we started using phones.

284
00:30:21.213 --> 00:30:25.037
But we're making quite a bit of progress.

285
00:30:25.037 --> 00:30:31.250
The refineries were getting really upset because of the information we were gathering.

286
00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:36.985
So they started doing more crooked stuff . . . more lies and more deceits.

287
00:30:36.985 --> 00:30:43.199
<v ARSENEAULT>When they saw y’all out there with the buckets, what would they do?

288
00:30:43.199 --> 00:30:44.632
<v VINCENT>They would panic.

289
00:30:44.632 --> 00:30:49.890
They would get their little crew out, some of their agents.

290
00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:53.235
They would call the police, sheriff department.

291
00:30:53.235 --> 00:31:05.662
But by that time we would have our sample and gone, because we had strategies set up to find out just where it was coming from,

292
00:31:05.662 --> 00:31:12.831
how to go in and get the sample as quick as we can and gone.

293
00:31:12.831 --> 00:31:16.654
So that's the way that would be working.

294
00:31:16.654 --> 00:31:19.044
We would move pretty fast.

295
00:31:19.044 --> 00:31:21.912
<v ARSENEAULT>So y’all had a plan beforehand?

296
00:31:21.912 --> 00:31:22.390
<v VINCENT>Yes.

297
00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:24.779
We had a working plan.

298
00:31:24.779 --> 00:31:29.559
Most times it would be at night, because that's when

299
00:31:29.559 --> 00:31:36.250
<c.p>they would dump their poison, at night, like a rainy night . . . stuff like that.

300
00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:45.331
So we would be out there in the rain and cold and whatever it took to get those samples.

301
00:31:45.331 --> 00:31:52.500
So a lot of times we would move, it would be mostly me and Shirley.

302
00:31:52.500 --> 00:32:03.493
The way we would do it is she would let me out at a certain spot and she would go down the road.

303
00:32:03.493 --> 00:32:09.706
By the time she'd go down the road, turn around and come back,

304
00:32:09.706 --> 00:32:25.000
I would have the sample and all I would have to do is just jump in the car with her and we'd take off and get it mailed off to them.

305
00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:27.450
<v ARSENEAULT>So how does it work?

306
00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:31.369
How does the bucket . . . how does that work?

307
00:32:31.369 --> 00:32:41.659
<v VINCENT>Well the bucket is . . . you got a little machine inside the bucket that draws the poison into, you got a bag.

308
00:32:41.659 --> 00:32:44.598
It draws it into the bag.

309
00:32:44.598 --> 00:32:48.028
It seals itself off in the bag.

310
00:32:48.028 --> 00:32:57.827
So whenever you take the bag, put it in the box and mail it to the address of the lab,

311
00:32:57.827 --> 00:33:06.647
and within a certain amount of time they would get the sample and send it back to us.

312
00:33:06.647 --> 00:33:13.996
So most of the times it would be at night or be a rainy day,

313
00:33:13.996 --> 00:33:22.325
and that's the kind of weather we'd be operating in to get samples on a regular basis.

314
00:33:22.325 --> 00:33:33.104
So we would have to be ready so if anybody calls us and say, "We got a smell over in this area,

315
00:33:33.104 --> 00:33:40.454
or over in that area," then that's where we would go to get our sample.

316
00:33:40.454 --> 00:33:44.373
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything else you remember about the bucket brigade?

317
00:33:44.373 --> 00:33:46.823
<v VINCENT>It was night and day.

318
00:33:46.823 --> 00:33:55.153
A lot of times we'd have to be in the car, ready, if we get a call.

319
00:33:55.153 --> 00:34:07.402
So a lot of times we would be sleeping in the car so we could be ready to move on out and get the sample.

320
00:34:07.402 --> 00:34:14.261
Sometimes we would be, maybe, two or three of us getting samples in different

321
00:34:14.261 --> 00:34:20.631
<c.p>places because we might get two or three different calls from different areas.

322
00:34:20.631 --> 00:34:24.060
So we were on the go regular.

323
00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you get involved?

324
00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:32.083
<v VINCENT>Well, I was already involved in the struggle.

325
00:34:32.083 --> 00:34:39.708
So I've always been interested in fighting for justice, truth, peace, freedom.

326
00:34:39.708 --> 00:34:42.250
It's in my blood.

327
00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:47.333
I'm a son of the Eli Vincent estate.

328
00:34:47.333 --> 00:34:58.771
And if you read the book of Eli, then you got an idea where I'm coming from [Laughs].

329
00:34:58.771 --> 00:35:08.302
Have you read the book of Eli? [Laughs] You can get it from the library.

330
00:35:08.302 --> 00:35:09.573
Yes so . . .

331
00:35:09.573 --> 00:35:13.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, that was your great-great-grandfather, right?

332
00:35:13.385 --> 00:35:14.021
<v VINCENT>Yes.

333
00:35:14.021 --> 00:35:21.646
So the only thing I can say is it's in my blood.

334
00:35:21.646 --> 00:35:24.188
It's in my genes.

335
00:35:24.188 --> 00:35:26.729
<v ARSENEAULT>It's in the DNA.

336
00:35:26.729 --> 00:35:28.000
<v VINCENT>Yes.

337
00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:31.395
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the first buyouts?

338
00:35:31.395 --> 00:35:35.469
What do you remember about those?

339
00:35:35.469 --> 00:35:37.506
<v VINCENT>It was hectic.

340
00:35:37.506 --> 00:35:42.259
They had the people against each other.

341
00:35:42.259 --> 00:35:47.012
I'm talking about it was chaos, really.

342
00:35:47.012 --> 00:35:57.198
And the people are still unhappy about what happened to them because they're in a

343
00:35:57.198 --> 00:36:01.272
<c.p>hole deeper than they were before.

344
00:36:01.272 --> 00:36:11.457
The buyout was bad, they had lawyers trying to get the people to sell out.

345
00:36:11.457 --> 00:36:16.210
I'm talking about it was a tragedy.

346
00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:21.642
The people are still pissed off about it.

347
00:36:21.642 --> 00:36:28.432
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the lawyers involved or anything like that?

348
00:36:28.432 --> 00:36:30.469
<v VINCENT>Not right off.

349
00:36:30.469 --> 00:36:38.617
But it was local lawyers who supposed to been representing the people.

350
00:36:38.617 --> 00:36:44.728
They were crossing the people for a little change.

351
00:36:44.728 --> 00:36:48.802
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the lawsuit in 1995?

352
00:36:48.802 --> 00:36:58.988
When they . . . When Condea Vista was taken to court, do you remember anything about that?

353
00:36:58.988 --> 00:37:05.099
<v VINCENT>I wasn't involved, but all of it was chaotic.

354
00:37:05.099 --> 00:37:09.852
All of it was betraying the community.

355
00:37:09.852 --> 00:37:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember how much was offered in that first buyout?

356
00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:20.681
<v VINCENT>No, not really.

357
00:37:20.681 --> 00:37:26.043
But it wasn't a fair amount.

358
00:37:26.043 --> 00:37:29.617
I do remember that.

359
00:37:29.617 --> 00:37:33.191
Everybody was really upset.

360
00:37:33.191 --> 00:37:44.809
They were threatening the lawyers who were involved with the sellout and whatnot.

361
00:37:44.809 --> 00:37:50.170
Everybody was at each other’s throats.

362
00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:57.319
<v ARSENEAULT>And it was the Bel Air area first?

363
00:37:57.319 --> 00:37:58.213
<v VINCENT>Yes.

364
00:37:58.213 --> 00:38:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

365
00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:08.099
<v VINCENT>They said it started in the Bel Air area and they came up to the tracks.

366
00:38:08.099 --> 00:38:08.605
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

367
00:38:08.605 --> 00:38:15.691
<v VINCENT>They said the spill didn't go across the tracks, or through the fence [laughs].

368
00:38:15.691 --> 00:38:19.235
<v ARSENEAULT>How do they know that, I wonder?

369
00:38:19.235 --> 00:38:20.753
<v VINCENT>Yes, good question.

370
00:38:20.753 --> 00:38:23.284
They knew they were lying.

371
00:38:23.284 --> 00:38:25.309
They knew that far.

372
00:38:25.309 --> 00:38:34.420
So that's . . . That was the big straw that broke the camel's back when it did that. [phone ringing]

373
00:38:34.420 --> 00:38:37.457
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you need us to pause?

374
00:38:37.457 --> 00:38:37.963
<v VINCENT>Yes.

375
00:38:37.963 --> 00:38:41.000
Excuse me. [break in tape]

376
00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:42.026
<v ARSENEAULT>Looks good.

377
00:38:42.026 --> 00:38:43.051
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Going on?

378
00:38:43.051 --> 00:38:44.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, we're on.

379
00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:49.718
Alright, so . . . Oh, sorry I probably should have told you.

380
00:38:49.718 --> 00:38:50.744
<v TANGLAO>That's okay.

381
00:38:50.744 --> 00:38:51.256
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

382
00:38:51.256 --> 00:38:55.872
So the first buyouts didn't come over the tracks.

383
00:38:55.872 --> 00:39:01.000
What do you . . . How are you involved with MEAN?

384
00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:04.886
<v VINCENT>I'm not involved with MEAN.

385
00:39:04.886 --> 00:39:14.213
MEAN ostracized Shirley and myself because we wouldn't go by the script.

386
00:39:14.213 --> 00:39:20.431
So we're not involved with MEAN any longer.

387
00:39:20.431 --> 00:39:30.535
MEAN had become weak and a sellout, as far as the community goes.

388
00:39:30.535 --> 00:39:38.307
They just reaching for crumbs, as far as I'm concerned.

389
00:39:38.307 --> 00:39:49.188
Because all of those moves that we've been involved with all over the country,

390
00:39:49.188 --> 00:40:00.069
some of them were in Detroit [Michigan], and Atlanta [Georgia], and you name it.

391
00:40:00.069 --> 00:40:11.728
But it's all involved in following the script . . . and we don't go by the script.

392
00:40:11.728 --> 00:40:17.946
<v ARSENEAULT>What was MEAN's goal when they first started?

393
00:40:17.946 --> 00:40:26.495
<v VINCENT>MEAN's goal was to make the refineries straighten their act up.

394
00:40:26.495 --> 00:40:29.604
But it never occurred,

395
00:40:29.604 --> 00:40:55.252
because they started throwing crumbs and people started going for the crumbs and going for the little trips that they'd go on so they can eat a meal and sleep in a hotel.

396
00:40:55.252 --> 00:40:59.916
But they haven't been making progress.

397
00:40:59.916 --> 00:41:11.574
That's been counterproductive, as far as I'm concerned and as far as the truth goes.

398
00:41:11.574 --> 00:41:13.906
They've been counterproductive.

399
00:41:13.906 --> 00:41:24.010
So the government and the refineries are in bed together, raping the communities.

400
00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:28.673
That's the bottom line right there.

401
00:41:28.673 --> 00:41:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So when MEAN first started, it's different than it was now?

402
00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:39.918
<v VINCENT>Yes, much different.

403
00:41:39.918 --> 00:41:43.754
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you first get involved?

404
00:41:43.754 --> 00:41:48.230
<v VINCENT>Must have been in the late '80s.

405
00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:53.984
That's when I started, I think, around that time.

406
00:41:53.984 --> 00:42:08.049
The '80s, '90s, that's when I started traveling with them, going to different places like Geneva, Switzerland and different places like that.

407
00:42:08.049 --> 00:42:21.475
But as I traveled and became more wise, I could see where it wasn't taking us anywhere but around in circles.

408
00:42:21.475 --> 00:42:35.541
So that's when I had to let them know that they're not handling the truth, so I couldn't go by their scripts.

409
00:42:35.541 --> 00:42:41.934
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y'all first meet when it first started out?

410
00:42:41.934 --> 00:42:48.967
<v VINCENT>Right here in Mossville, in the community at the community center.

411
00:42:48.967 --> 00:42:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And now where does MEAN . . . where do they meet now?

412
00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:05.485
<v VINCENT>I don't know where they have clandestine meetings . . . here, there,

413
00:43:05.485 --> 00:43:20.662
or wherever. [Laughs] So they're like hiding their selves and doing what they instructed to do.

414
00:43:20.662 --> 00:43:28.250
<v ARSENEAULT>Are there any other similar groups in Mossville?

415
00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:43.426
<v VINCENT>Yes and no . . . because most of the people are working without the city being involved, dictating.

416
00:43:43.426 --> 00:43:52.912
<v ARSENEAULT>So what do you remember about any of the spills?

417
00:43:52.912 --> 00:44:06.191
<v VINCENT>I remember that we got a lot of good reading from the Bucket Brigades.

418
00:44:06.191 --> 00:44:15.676
But I don't know what they did with the information.

419
00:44:15.676 --> 00:44:26.110
I don't know how that was being handled at that time.

420
00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:39.390
But we got a lot of good readings that they're really poisoning the people.

421
00:44:39.390 --> 00:45:05.000
But who handles that after they get the good readings is another question, because I don't know if it's mercenaries who are handling it or what.

422
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:08.557
<v ARSENEAULT>So you have all this information.

423
00:45:08.557 --> 00:45:10.929
What happens to it?

424
00:45:10.929 --> 00:45:25.157
<v VINCENT>That's a good question, too, because the ones who are supposed to be representing the community are mainly the ones who handle the information.

425
00:45:25.157 --> 00:45:33.457
So they . . . I don't know what they do with them when they got it.

426
00:45:33.457 --> 00:45:47.686
I don't know who get it from them, but I do know that I gave a testimony at a meeting we had one time.

427
00:45:47.686 --> 00:45:50.650
It was called a tribunal.

428
00:45:50.650 --> 00:45:55.986
The testimony I gave was maybe about an hour.

429
00:45:55.986 --> 00:46:07.843
And I was supposed to get a copy of this testimony before this tribunal, and I haven't got it yet.

430
00:46:07.843 --> 00:46:10.214
That's been years ago.

431
00:46:10.214 --> 00:46:19.107
That happened in New Orleans [Louisiana] under the organization that's supposed to be representing Mossville.

432
00:46:19.107 --> 00:46:25.036
Monique Harden was one of the ones who's over that.

433
00:46:25.036 --> 00:46:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is she a lawyer?

434
00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:30.343
<v VINCENT>She's a lawyer, yes.

435
00:46:30.343 --> 00:46:32.686
She's an environmentalist lawyer.

436
00:46:32.686 --> 00:46:39.716
She's one of the ones who went to Geneva, Switzerland with us.

437
00:46:39.716 --> 00:46:44.402
So it’s a whole lot of unanswered questions

438
00:46:44.402 --> 00:46:44.988
<c.p>involved.

439
00:46:44.988 --> 00:46:51.432
I don't know when or if the truth is coming out.

440
00:46:51.432 --> 00:46:54.947
But there's a lot of unanswered.

441
00:46:54.947 --> 00:47:03.734
<v ARSENEAULT>I kind of want to know what it's like living next to so many plants.

442
00:47:03.734 --> 00:47:09.006
Like, what are some of the smells, or noises?

443
00:47:09.006 --> 00:47:16.036
<v VINCENT>Smells and noises are like . . . The noises are like a thunder storm.

444
00:47:16.036 --> 00:47:18.379
That's a refinery now.

445
00:47:18.379 --> 00:47:23.065
It be blowing off wherever they blowing off.

446
00:47:23.065 --> 00:47:31.266
They be dumping stuff at night, especially wet nights when it's raining and stuff.

447
00:47:31.266 --> 00:47:35.953
<v ARSENEAULT>Why do they do it on wet nights?

448
00:47:35.953 --> 00:47:38.882
<v VINCENT>Because it's harder to detect.

449
00:47:38.882 --> 00:47:50.012
Raining and whatnot, not many people going to be around out and they're not going to see as much.

450
00:47:50.012 --> 00:47:55.870
So the rain helps them mask the . . . whatever it is.

451
00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:59.970
It could be a fire, smoke, noise.

452
00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:07.000
All that be happening at night to cover up their wickedness.

453
00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:10.094
<v ARSENEAULT>Is there any other like alerts?

454
00:48:10.094 --> 00:48:18.859
<v VINCENT>Every now and then you might hear some . . . like a siren, or you might hear some chatter.

455
00:48:18.859 --> 00:48:22.469
But you can't tell what it is.

456
00:48:22.469 --> 00:48:33.812
All you know is it's just chatter at night, and mostly the bad nights is when they really do what they do.

457
00:48:33.812 --> 00:48:40.000
So nice nights and whatnot, they're not going to be doing much.

458
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:43.094
<v ARSENEAULT>What about like an evacuation plan?

459
00:48:43.094 --> 00:48:45.156
Does the community have . . .

460
00:48:45.156 --> 00:48:55.469
<v VINCENT>That's bullshit, because evacuation plan, a lot of times they have the gates locked; you can't even go that way.

461
00:48:55.469 --> 00:49:00.109
They have security out there sending you another way.

462
00:49:00.109 --> 00:49:07.844
They rarely have the evacuation business going on because they don't . . . [knocking on the door]

463
00:49:07.844 --> 00:49:10.422
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, should we pause it?

464
00:49:10.422 --> 00:49:13.000
<v TANGLAO>Should we pause it?

465
00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:14.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, pause it.

466
00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:16.267
<v TANGLAO>Okay. [break in tape]

467
00:49:16.267 --> 00:49:17.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, go.

468
00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:25.133
Alright, so a question I did want to ask you, kind of going back to the activism.

469
00:49:25.133 --> 00:49:28.400
Did you ever work with Debra Ramirez?

470
00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:28.867
<v VINCENT>Yes.

471
00:49:28.867 --> 00:49:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What can you tell us about some of her work?

472
00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:36.219
<v VINCENT>Yes, she's a go-getter.

473
00:49:36.219 --> 00:49:40.658
We've been on quite a few missions together.

474
00:49:40.658 --> 00:49:51.200
I think a lot of those missions we were on together, like Atlanta, Detroit . . . I don't know how many.

475
00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:58.413
But yes, she's definitely a go-getter as far as being a freedom fighter.

476
00:49:58.413 --> 00:50:01.187
<v ARSENEAULT>How did she get involved?

477
00:50:01.187 --> 00:50:04.516
How did she first get involved?

478
00:50:04.516 --> 00:50:11.174
<v VINCENT>Well, she was living right there across the street from the refinery.

479
00:50:11.174 --> 00:50:12.839
<v ARSENEAULT>In Bel Air?

480
00:50:12.839 --> 00:50:13.394
<v VINCENT>Yes.

481
00:50:13.394 --> 00:50:18.387
She was a little girl they were living there.

482
00:50:18.387 --> 00:50:22.271
She would run around barefoot and whatnot.

483
00:50:22.271 --> 00:50:26.710
Her father was a good friend of mine.

484
00:50:26.710 --> 00:50:30.594
He used to work for the parish.

485
00:50:30.594 --> 00:50:39.471
But that's when they really start realizing the poison that they were receiving from the refineries.

486
00:50:39.471 --> 00:50:45.574
So she was a little girl when she started recognizing it.

487
00:50:45.574 --> 00:50:55.006
And she was one of the first ones who really started standing up and fighting against it.

488
00:50:55.006 --> 00:51:00.000
Debra is . . . she's a freedom fighter for sure.

489
00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.672
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the first things that she did?

490
00:51:04.672 --> 00:51:07.942
<v VINCENT>She started exposing the refineries, the spill.

491
00:51:07.942 --> 00:51:13.080
She was one of the first ones involved in the spill.

492
00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:21.956
Just how . . . because she was involved before I got involved, because they were living right there at the plants.

493
00:51:21.956 --> 00:51:30.832
So that's when it really started . . . I think it really started with her family and her neighbors right there,

494
00:51:30.832 --> 00:51:33.635
just about living in the refineries.

495
00:51:33.635 --> 00:51:35.504
So . . . Let me see.

496
00:51:35.504 --> 00:51:37.839
David . . . what's his last name?

497
00:51:37.839 --> 00:51:40.642
Y'all probably interviewed his family, too.

498
00:51:40.642 --> 00:51:44.380
He died in a fire in his house.

499
00:51:44.380 --> 00:51:46.715
He was one of the . . .

500
00:51:46.715 --> 00:51:48.584
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, the Prince family?

501
00:51:48.584 --> 00:51:49.051
<v VINCENT>Yes.

502
00:51:49.051 --> 00:51:53.255
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't think I've interviewed anyone from that family.

503
00:51:53.255 --> 00:51:53.723
<v VINCENT>No?

504
00:51:53.723 --> 00:52:04.000
Well he was a good friend of mine, and he used to travel with us on a lot of these missions.

505
00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:05.378
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened to him?

506
00:52:05.378 --> 00:52:09.167
<v VINCENT>He was cooking in his house and a fire broke out.

507
00:52:09.167 --> 00:52:11.578
He got burned up in his house.

508
00:52:11.578 --> 00:52:14.678
That's questionable, too, because he was really a fighter.

509
00:52:14.678 --> 00:52:21.222
He used to park his vehicle in the driveway of the refinery so they couldn't go in and out.

510
00:52:21.222 --> 00:52:24.667
They'd call the police on him and all of this.

511
00:52:24.667 --> 00:52:28.456
David Prince was one of the principal persons in the fight.

512
00:52:28.456 --> 00:52:31.556
<v ARSENEAULT>He was from the Bel Air area as well?

513
00:52:31.556 --> 00:52:32.244
<v VINCENT>[Agrees] Yes.

514
00:52:32.244 --> 00:52:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And he was close with Ms. Debra?

515
00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:35.583
<v VINCENT>Yes.

516
00:52:35.583 --> 00:52:40.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Kind of on the frontlines out there, I guess?

517
00:52:40.833 --> 00:52:41.417
<v VINCENT>Yes.

518
00:52:41.417 --> 00:52:52.500
So that was really strange the way he died, because we had just came back from a mission somewhere.

519
00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:54.833
I don't remember where.

520
00:52:54.833 --> 00:53:03.000
But we stayed together and that's when we really got to know each other.

521
00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:06.500
We stayed in the same apartment.

522
00:53:06.500 --> 00:53:07.083
Yes.

523
00:53:07.083 --> 00:53:19.333
So you had quite a few who were true freedom fighters, but then you had a lot who were traitors, too,

524
00:53:19.333 --> 00:53:32.750
because somebody in their family had a little plant job and they didn't want them to lose their job . . . carrying on like that.

525
00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:38.583
So yes, David Prince was one of the key people.

526
00:53:38.583 --> 00:53:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any other mysterious deaths or anything like that?

527
00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:52.235
<v VINCENT>Well a lot of them were mysterious deaths because they were chemical-related.

528
00:53:52.235 --> 00:54:04.294
They were dying and didn't know they were being killed because the management don't tell them what they're dealing with,

529
00:54:04.294 --> 00:54:12.132
what kind of chemical, what they need to use to protect their selves.

530
00:54:12.132 --> 00:54:18.162
Back then, they didn't care nothing about protecting the workers.

531
00:54:18.162 --> 00:54:27.206
The only thing they cared about was getting the work done by any means necessary.

532
00:54:27.206 --> 00:54:33.838
That mean you have to be exposed to chemicals and die.

533
00:54:33.838 --> 00:54:36.250
You know we're expendable.

534
00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:43.485
It's always somebody in line waiting to get the job you got.

535
00:54:43.485 --> 00:54:47.103
So that's the way they were.

536
00:54:47.103 --> 00:55:01.574
So be careful about trying to get a plant job. [Laughs] Be careful what you ask for; you just might get it all. [Laughs]

537
00:55:01.574 --> 00:55:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the coverage, the media coverage, of Mossville?

538
00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:22.074
<v VINCENT>Media coverage is lightweight, because they care about the government and they don't want to make no noise or no waves dealing with the system.

539
00:55:22.074 --> 00:55:29.309
So they just barely touch on whatever they have to touch on.

540
00:55:29.309 --> 00:55:35.941
As far as true coverage, no they're not thinking about that.

541
00:55:35.941 --> 00:55:42.574
<c.p>They're thinking about the kickbacks they're getting from the big boys.

542
00:55:42.574 --> 00:55:46.191
What's the name of that paper?

543
00:55:46.191 --> 00:55:48.000
American Press.

544
00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:50.559
<v ARSENEAULT>The Lake Charles American Press.

545
00:55:50.559 --> 00:55:51.071
<v VINCENT>Yes.

546
00:55:51.071 --> 00:55:52.606
<v ARSENEAULT>And then KPLC.

547
00:55:52.606 --> 00:55:54.142
<v VINCENT>Yes, they're weak.

548
00:55:54.142 --> 00:55:57.724
<v ARSENEAULT>What about specials that have been done?

549
00:55:57.724 --> 00:56:04.890
<v VINCENT>Oh yes, they got some specials like Toxic Town Mossville by Dr. Sanjay Gupta.

550
00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:07.961
A lot of those are good.

551
00:56:07.961 --> 00:56:16.661
But the other ones, the little roody poo newspapers and whatnot, they're not concerned with the community.

552
00:56:16.661 --> 00:56:23.827
They're concerned with the refineries and the kickbacks they can get from the refineries.

553
00:56:23.827 --> 00:56:27.409
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I think we're at an hour.

554
00:56:27.409 --> 00:56:32.528
I know there's a lot . . . This is a heavy topic.

555
00:56:32.528 --> 00:56:44.299
I was going to ask you, were there any . . . This could be the last question, and then we can kind of wrap up.

556
00:56:44.299 --> 00:56:53.000
Where there any like programs offered to the community, like education programs or anything like that?

557
00:56:53.000 --> 00:57:04.333
<v VINCENT>The only education programs they're offering for the community is you can get you a plant job.

558
00:57:04.333 --> 00:57:07.667
You want a plant job?

559
00:57:07.667 --> 00:57:11.667
Come on and take these classes.

560
00:57:11.667 --> 00:57:15.667
Other than that, it ain't happening.

561
00:57:15.667 --> 00:57:29.667
They might give some little roody-poo, something like they have some balloons and some little toys for children to play with.

562
00:57:29.667 --> 00:57:43.667
But other than that, nothing significant, nothing that's going to empower the people with knowledge, how to straighten this mess out.

563
00:57:43.667 --> 00:57:45.667
That's not happening.

564
00:57:45.667 --> 00:57:51.667
Everything represents . . . We can get you a plant job.

565
00:57:51.667 --> 00:57:57.000
So by the time you're sixty, you dead.

566
00:57:57.000 --> 00:58:09.667
And you don't get none of that money they're supposed to be saving to give you when you retire.

567
00:58:09.667 --> 00:58:22.333
The average person I've seen has got about, maybe, three or four checks from retirement before they passed away.

568
00:58:22.333 --> 00:58:27.000
I know one person got one check.

569
00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:34.333
The next check he was dead, so he couldn't cash it.

570
00:58:34.333 --> 00:58:41.000
That's what most people here, that's what's happening to them.

571
00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:46.333
They don't get none of their retirement money.

572
00:58:46.333 --> 00:58:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever any like education classes on like health and risks . . . anything like that?

573
00:58:57.000 --> 00:58:58.177
<v VINCENT>Not real.

574
00:58:58.177 --> 00:59:08.774
Most of them are fake business and most of them are chosen by the ones who running it.

575
00:59:08.774 --> 00:59:17.605
They get a chosen few who's going to go by the script and that's it.

576
00:59:17.605 --> 00:59:27.024
Just like most people with the jobs that come in are people who don't live here.

577
00:59:27.024 --> 00:59:42.331
So most of Mossville people who be trying to get jobs at the refineries, they don't get them because they have people from out of state.

578
00:59:42.331 --> 00:59:48.218
<v ARSENEAULT>So they're not even from here, they're from somewhere else.

579
00:59:48.218 --> 00:59:51.750
<v VINCENT>So corruption here is the key.

580
00:59:51.750 --> 01:00:02.347
Just like [Edwin] Edwards just got out of prison, and he ain't scared to go back, either [Laughs].

581
01:00:02.347 --> 01:00:07.645
He still do the same thing he's been doing.

582
01:00:07.645 --> 01:00:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He's a character.

583
01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:15.303
<v VINCENT>Going to the casinos, spending the people's money, and talking shit and lying.

584
01:00:15.303 --> 01:00:17.342
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever meet him?

585
01:00:17.342 --> 01:00:19.382
<v VINCENT>No, I didn't meet him.

586
01:00:19.382 --> 01:00:23.461
But I've seen a lot of his work though [Laughs].

587
01:00:23.461 --> 01:00:28.355
<v ARSENEAULT>I know I think he was involved in some of the Mossville . . .

588
01:00:28.355 --> 01:00:29.987
<v VINCENT>Oh yes, of course.

589
01:00:29.987 --> 01:00:37.737
Matter of fact, he was involved in the casino business where they had this black section of the graveyard.

590
01:00:37.737 --> 01:00:40.184
This casino right over here, little . . .

591
01:00:40.184 --> 01:00:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>L'Auberge?

592
01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:42.527
<v VINCENT>No, the other one.

593
01:00:42.527 --> 01:00:43.673
<v ARSENEAULT>The Golden Nugget?

594
01:00:43.673 --> 01:00:46.345
<v VINCENT>What's the one right on this side?

595
01:00:46.345 --> 01:00:46.727
L'Auberge . . .

596
01:00:46.727 --> 01:00:47.873
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't know.

597
01:00:47.873 --> 01:00:50.545
I know L'Auberge is the big one.

598
01:00:50.545 --> 01:00:52.455
<v VINCENT>Yes, not the big one.

599
01:00:52.455 --> 01:00:57.800
The one with the bird, right at the foot of the bridge right there.

600
01:00:57.800 --> 01:00:58.182
<v ARSENEAULT>Paradise?

601
01:00:58.182 --> 01:00:59.709
No. I don't know.

602
01:00:59.709 --> 01:01:00.091
Capri!

603
01:01:00.091 --> 01:01:02.000
<v VINCENT>Yes, Isle of Capri.

604
01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:02.515
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

605
01:01:02.515 --> 01:01:08.175
<v VINCENT>They had a graveyard right there where they built that casino.

606
01:01:08.175 --> 01:01:13.835
They had a white section graveyard and a black section graveyard.

607
01:01:13.835 --> 01:01:22.583
They finally asked the white section to do whatever they had to do, move it or whatever.

608
01:01:22.583 --> 01:01:27.214
But the black section, they just . . . That's history, too.

609
01:01:27.214 --> 01:01:30.301
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened to the black section?

610
01:01:30.301 --> 01:01:32.874
They just built over it?

611
01:01:32.874 --> 01:01:34.932
<v VINCENT>That's a question, too.

612
01:01:34.932 --> 01:01:37.505
We don't know what happened.

613
01:01:37.505 --> 01:01:46.252
The blacks didn't get compensated for their ancestors who were buried there, but the white section did.

614
01:01:46.252 --> 01:01:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Edwards ever involved . . . Edwin . . . Governor Edwin Edwards, was he involved in getting water to Mossville?

615
01:01:55.000 --> 01:01:56.600
<v VINCENT>I don't know.

616
01:01:56.600 --> 01:01:57.133
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

617
01:01:57.133 --> 01:01:58.733
I'm just curious.

618
01:01:58.733 --> 01:02:03.000
<v VINCENT>[laughs] If it was anything involving crime . . .

619
01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:03.835
<v ARSENEAULT>And corruption.

620
01:02:03.835 --> 01:02:05.506
<v VINCENT>Yes. [Laughs] He was.

621
01:02:05.506 --> 01:02:07.595
I'll put it like that.

622
01:02:07.595 --> 01:02:08.848
<v ARSENEAULT>Him and his . . .

623
01:02:08.848 --> 01:02:10.937
<v VINCENT>Because that's all I heard.

624
01:02:10.937 --> 01:02:12.190
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . twenty year-old wife.

625
01:02:12.190 --> 01:02:12.608
<v VINCENT>Yes.

626
01:02:12.608 --> 01:02:19.291
That's all I heard about when I moved here was him and what he was doing.

627
01:02:19.291 --> 01:02:21.380
It was nothing but corruption.

628
01:02:21.380 --> 01:02:28.481
The ones who were with him were one of the mercenaries would get a couple of dollars.

629
01:02:28.481 --> 01:02:29.734
Simple as that.

630
01:02:29.734 --> 01:02:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone in the community kind of work for him or anything like that?

631
01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:37.515
<v VINCENT>I'm sure, yes.

632
01:02:37.515 --> 01:02:39.030
Montgomery . . . Valery Montgomery.

633
01:02:39.030 --> 01:02:51.152
Those kind of people used to work for him because they used to go around the community to tell them who to vote for.

634
01:02:51.152 --> 01:02:53.677
They get a little kickback.

635
01:02:53.677 --> 01:02:58.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would they tell the community to vote for?

636
01:02:58.222 --> 01:03:00.242
<v VINCENT>Whoever they were told.

637
01:03:00.242 --> 01:03:09.333
In other words, if it was Edwards, that's who he would . . . whoever the fat cats with a dollar.

638
01:03:09.333 --> 01:03:11.354
That's who'd they'd vote.

639
01:03:11.354 --> 01:03:12.869
Simple as that.

640
01:03:12.869 --> 01:03:22.970
I couldn't believe it when I got here how far Lousy-ana is behind other states like Washington, D.C. and whatnot.

641
01:03:22.970 --> 01:03:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you call it "Lousy-ana"?

642
01:03:26.000 --> 01:03:27.100
<v VINCENT>Lousy [Laughs].

643
01:03:27.100 --> 01:03:28.200
Really lousy.

644
01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:34.800
I mean, you know it's run by . . . what do they call it?

645
01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:38.650
What do they call this system here?

646
01:03:38.650 --> 01:03:40.300
The Napoleonic Laws?

647
01:03:40.300 --> 01:03:41.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, yes.

648
01:03:41.400 --> 01:03:43.600
<v VINCENT>You know about that?

649
01:03:43.600 --> 01:03:46.900
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees] It's still under Napoleonic Law.

650
01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:47.450
<v VINCENT>Yes.

651
01:03:47.450 --> 01:03:51.300
<v ARSENEAULT>You can't eat cherry pie on Sunday.

